  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "One English phrase covers caring for a child, a sick person, an animal, and a thing or a task; many languages use a different verb for each.",
    "grammar": "Look after is never separated: look after the baby, look after her, never look her after. It takes its object directly, with no second preposition: look after someone, not look after of someone. The passive is possible: the garden is well looked after.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "In British English after has the long ɑː vowel and no r sound; in American English it has æ and ends in an r sound. Look is short, lʊk."
  },
